---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import CaseStudyNav from '../../components/CaseStudyNav.astro';
import '../../styles/case-study.css';

import orchestration from '../../assets/images/claude-orchestration-layer-diagram.png';
import journeyMap1 from '../../assets/images/journey-map-1.png';
import travelWireframes from '../../assets/images/travel-sd-wireframes.png';
---
<Layout title="AI Tooling | Andrew Smith" description="How AI tools reduce overhead and accelerate discovery across the design and product process.">
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to content</a>
  <div class="case-page">
    <SiteHeader />
    <main id="main-content">

      <section class="case-hero">
        <p class="eyebrow"><a class="breadcrumb-link" href="/portfolio/work">Work</a><span class="breadcrumb-separator">/</span><span>AI Tooling</span></p>
        <h1 class="display-title">AI in the design and product process.</h1>
        <p class="hero-text">AI tools are most useful when they reduce the overhead that competes with the work that requires judgment. Synthesis, documentation, spec generation, pattern identification — when AI handles these, the time that is freed up goes toward discovery, testing, and the decisions that actually move product quality forward.</p>
        <p class="hero-subtext">Faster discovery means the business can validate or redirect product decisions faster. That is a business outcome argument, not a craft efficiency argument.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="meta-strip">
        <div><p class="meta-label">Scope</p><p class="meta-value">Research, design, delivery, knowledge management</p></div>
        <div><p class="meta-label">Role</p><p class="meta-value">UX Manager, Senior UX Design Lead</p></div>
        <div><p class="meta-label">Tools</p><p class="meta-value">Claude, Figma AI, journey management AI, UserTesting</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="lead-image-section">
        <Image class="lead-image" src={orchestration} alt="Experience Design operating model showing AI orchestration layer connecting all tools and knowledge sources" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </section>

      <section class="summary-section">
        <p class="section-kicker">The Principle</p>
        <h2>Faster discovery means better product decisions, sooner.</h2>
        <div class="summary-body">
          <p>When AI compresses the time it takes to synthesise research, generate journey map structures from raw notes, or produce component documentation from a design file, the gain is not just hours saved. It is earlier access to the insight that would have taken a week to surface manually — which means product decisions get better information sooner, and the cost of being wrong about a direction is lower.</p>
          <p>This framing shapes how AI has been used across the team's process. Not as a way to do design faster at the expense of quality, but as a way to reduce the administrative overhead that competes with quality.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-content">
          <p class="section-kicker">Research Synthesis</p>
          <h2>From raw sessions to actionable insights faster.</h2>
          <p>Research synthesis is one of the most time-consuming parts of discovery — reading session transcripts, tagging observations, identifying patterns across participants, and turning raw notes into a coherent problem statement. AI tools now assist with all of this.</p>
          <p>The practical outcome: a research round that previously took several days to synthesise can be turned around in hours. The designer spends their time reviewing and refining AI-generated patterns rather than building them from scratch. The quality of the insight is the same. The time to action is significantly shorter.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="section-image">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={journeyMap1} alt="Journey explorer showing live metrics and AI-surfaced patterns feeding into prioritised solutions" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-content">
          <p class="section-kicker">Rapid Prototyping</p>
          <h2>Testing ideas before committing to build.</h2>
          <p>AI-assisted prototyping tools can scan a live product and generate a working replica with a new feature integrated — so users test in context rather than against an abstract mockup. The result is more accurate feedback about whether the design works in the real environment.</p>
          <p>Figma's AI tools assist with variant generation, accessibility checking, and spec annotation — reducing the documentation overhead that would otherwise compete with design and testing time.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="section-image">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={travelWireframes} alt="Travel booking wireframes showing rapid prototype flow across search, detail, and payment screens" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <div class="section-content">
          <p class="section-kicker">Orchestration Layer</p>
          <h2>Institutional knowledge made accessible across a distributed team.</h2>
          <p>As the Experience Design team reduced in size, institutional knowledge became a risk. Individual designers knew things that were not documented. When someone left, that knowledge went with them.</p>
          <p>The orchestration layer addressed this by connecting the design system documentation, journey maps, research repository, live products, and internal standards — WCAG guidelines, solution architecture, brand requirements, legal constraints — into a single queryable source via a Slackbot. A designer in a product squad could ask a question in the channel they were already working in and get a synthesised answer from all available sources rather than searching across four tools manually.</p>
          <p>The outcome: a smaller team maintaining the same delivery quality and knowledge access because the institutional knowledge was finally accessible rather than locked in individual heads or buried in folders.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="section-image">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={orchestration} alt="Orchestration layer architecture showing business inputs, design tools, AI knowledge layer, and delivered outputs" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </section>

      <CaseStudyNav />
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>
